# Commander Cline Rules

Cline is an alternate coder backend that does not read `.claude/agents/coder.md`.
This file encodes the non-negotiable invariants that govern Cline-dispatched coder
runs. These rules are identical to those in `.claude/agents/coder.md` — there is ONE
set of coder invariants that applies equally to both Claude Code and Cline harnesses.

When this file and `coder.md` diverge, both must be updated to stay in sync.
See the note at the end of `CLAUDE.md` and `coder.md`.

---

## Merge Boundary — NEVER merge to develop or sprint branches (CRITICAL)

Your responsibility ends at pushing the feature branch. You must NOT merge to
the target branch by any means:

- **No `git merge`** — do not run git merge commands
- **No direct push to develop/master/sprint branches** — only push to `feature/<N>-*`
- **No `gh pr merge`** — do not approve, merge, or interact with pull requests
- **No `scripts/finish_feature.py`** — that is exclusively the Tester's job

Merging is done by the Tester agent via `scripts/finish_feature.py` after tests pass.
If you merge the branch yourself, the ticket will be stuck in an inconsistent state
and the UAT label will be skipped.

---

## Branch Naming Convention

Always create feature branches using `scripts/start_feature.py`:

```
python3 scripts/start_feature.py --issue <N> --base-branch <base>
```

Feature branch format: `feature/<N>-<slug>` (kebab-case, includes issue number)

Push the branch to origin immediately after creation.

---

## No Direct Push to Protected Branches

- Never push directly to `develop`, `master`, or any sprint branches
- All work lives on your assigned `feature/<N>-<slug>` branch
- After pushing the feature branch, your job is done — mark the ticket SIT and comment
- The Tester merges it into the base branch after tests pass

---

## Read AGENTS.md Files Before Coding

Before touching any code, identify which directories your work will touch.
For every implicated directory that has an `AGENTS.md`, read it immediately:

- `apps/dashboard/AGENTS.md`
- `apps/dashboard/routers/AGENTS.md`
- `apps/dashboard/static/AGENTS.md`
- `services/sprint_manager/AGENTS.md`
- `scripts/AGENTS.md`

Log which files you loaded:

```
[AGENTS.md] Loaded: apps/dashboard/AGENTS.md, services/sprint_manager/AGENTS.md
```

These files contain directory-specific danger zones and conventions. Skipping
this step leads to repeated mistakes.

---

## Stay Within Assigned Issue Scope

- Implement **exactly** what the Acceptance Criteria say — nothing more
- If an AC item requires code, implement it fully
- If you spot unrelated bugs or refactoring opportunities, do NOT fix them
  in this branch — file separate issues instead
- Do not introduce new patterns unless the AC requires it — follow existing
  conventions strictly

---

## Run Tests and Confirm Green Before Declaring Done

Before moving the ticket to SIT:

1. Run the test suite: `python -m pytest tests/ -x -q`
2. Confirm all tests pass (newly added tests AND existing tests)
3. Do not commit test files that are skipped or xfailed to hide failures
4. Re-read every AC item and verify you have implemented it
5. If something is missing, fix it and push again
6. Only then move the ticket to SIT via `update_ticket.py`

---

## Do Not Edit Critical Project Files

Do not modify:

- `.claude/agents/*` — agent definitions (read-only)
- `.commander/sprint.yaml` — sprint config (read-only)
- Label/transition logic in `sprint_manager.py` — merge automation (read-only)
- Any files outside your issue's scope

Use the official scripts (`start_feature.py`, `update_ticket.py`) for label changes.
Do not directly edit labels with `gh issue edit` or other tools.

---

## Self-Check Against Acceptance Criteria

Step 7 of the workflow (before SIT): re-read every AC item and confirm you
have implemented it. If you find something is missing, fix it and push again
before marking SIT.

---

## Workflow Summary

1. Create branch: `start_feature.py --issue <N>`
2. Read ticket: `gh issue view <N>`
3. Load AGENTS.md files for all implicated directories
4. Implement exactly per AC, following existing conventions
5. Commit in logical chunks with good messages
6. Push to feature branch: `git push origin feature/<N>-<slug>`
7. Re-read all AC items and verify coverage
8. Move to SIT: `update_ticket.py --issue <N> --status sit`
9. Comment with summary: `comment_ticket.py --issue <N> --body "..."`
10. Stop. The Tester takes over. Do not merge, do not push again.
